WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.668
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Welcome to
"Google Cloud Drawing Board,"

00:00:02.668 --> 00:00:04.650
where we doodle our
way through the cloud.

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:08.100
Today's topic-- what
is Cloud Operations?

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:10.380
This video is divided
into chapters.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:11.910
Watch the full
video, or skip ahead

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:13.695
to any new section
of your choice.

00:00:13.695 --> 00:00:16.200
You know the time when you
want to see how your cloud

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.180
deployment is behaving
or if something's

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:21.523
broken in production and
your users might be impacted?

00:00:21.523 --> 00:00:23.190
You are scrambling
because there are too

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:26.710
many tools to look through to
find and resolve the issue.

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:29.340
That's where Cloud
Operations comes in.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:31.890
It's a suite of products
to monitor, troubleshoot,

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:34.500
and operate your
services at scale,

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.590
enabling your DevOps,
SREs, or IT operations

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:41.430
teams to utilize the
Google's SRE best practices.

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:43.590
It offers integrated
capabilities

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:46.740
for monitoring, logging,
and advanced observability

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:50.010
services, like Trace,
Debugger, and Profiler.

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:53.610
The end-to-end operations suite
includes capturing signals,

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:56.280
monitoring systems,
managing incidents,

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:59.400
troubleshooting issues
with built-in telemetry,

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:03.700
out-of-the-box dashboards,
recommendations, and more.

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:07.170
So let's see how does
Cloud Operations work.

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:10.290
First, Cloud Logging, which
is a fully managed and highly

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:12.690
scalable service that
aggregates log data

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:15.210
from all your infrastructure
and applications

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:17.190
into a single location.

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:19.980
It collects the log data
from Google Cloud Services

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:21.120
automatically.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:24.170
And you can feed custom logs
through Cloud Logging agent,

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:27.190
open-source Fluentd, or the API.

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:29.280
It gives you complete
control to decide

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:32.700
how and where to store these
logs, including keeping them

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:36.390
in Cloud Logging, exporting
to Cloud Storage for archival,

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:39.300
BigQuery for analytics,
or streaming them

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:42.330
via Cloud Pub/Sub to a
third-party destination.

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:44.940
Log Viewer gives you
powerful capabilities

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:48.090
to filter the logs, convert
them to log-based metrics

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:51.840
for monitoring, alerting,
analyzing, and visualizing.

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.480
Cloud Monitoring
provides observability

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:56.310
across your apps
and infrastructure

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:58.590
regardless of where it is--

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:01.470
Google Cloud, on prem
or in other clouds.

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:04.050
It supports a variety
of metrics integrations

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:08.370
and allows you to define custom
metrics unique to your use case

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:11.910
and even send those metrics
to external systems.

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:15.540
Using the Metrics Explorer
and Monitoring Query Language,

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:18.240
you can analyze these
metrics on the fly,

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:20.670
identifying
correlations, and easily

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:23.620
add the corresponding
charts to a dashboard.

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:26.580
You can use out-of-the-box or
custom-built dashboards to get

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:29.490
a consolidated view of the
health of your infrastructure

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:34.320
service or application
and easily spot anomalies.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:36.810
But we know that you cannot
sit around all day looking

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:40.230
at dashboards, so Cloud
Monitoring provides alerting,

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:43.920
where you can create policies
to alert on performance metrics,

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.140
uptime checks, and
service-level indicators.

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:50.620
Now let's focus our attention on
advanced observability feature

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:51.450
Trace.

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:54.240
Trace provides
visualization and analysis

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:57.120
to understand request
flow, service topology,

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:59.160
and latency issues in your app.

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:01.920
Debugger allows you
to inspect the state

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:05.160
of running applications after
deployment without needing

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:06.900
to stop or slow it down.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:10.770
Profiler continuously analyzes
your code's performance

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:13.770
on each service so that
you can improve its speed

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:15.300
and reduce your cost.

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:18.720
And it is designed to run in
production with effectively

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:20.560
no performance impact.

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:23.700
While Trace is used to track
relationships and latency

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:26.520
between services,
Profiler tracks

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:30.120
this across the individual
functions in your code base.

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:33.390
And Debugger helps find
the root cause from method

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:36.100
to the specific
problematic piece of code.

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:38.820
Now, you can use the
Cloud Console or the API

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:40.680
to use Cloud Operations tools.

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:43.470
All these tools offer a
very generous free tier

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:45.780
to make it easy for
you to get started.

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:48.060
From the security
perspective, all your data

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:50.720
is encrypted at
rest and in transit.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:53.430
Your security-focused audit
logs are automatically

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:56.910
available in Cloud Logging,
telling you who did what,

00:03:56.910 --> 00:03:58.410
where, and when.

00:03:58.410 --> 00:04:00.630
And with Access
Transparency, you

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:03.150
can always stay in compliance
because it captures

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:06.270
the actions Google personnel
takes while offering you

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:07.470
support.

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:10.110
I bet that keeping your
applications up and running

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:12.360
and making sure your
customers are happy

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:14.500
is your number-one priority.

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:17.579
And Cloud Operations
helps you do just that.

00:04:17.579 --> 00:04:19.740
By using service-level
objectives that

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:23.220
work across all application
types and cloud environments

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:25.920
or using error reporting
to identify bugs

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:27.990
in your applications,
Cloud Operations

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:31.710
provides your ops teams with
the out of the box observability

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:35.070
to monitor your infrastructure
and your applications.

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:36.520
Want to get started?

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:37.650
Check out the free trial.

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:40.400
[MUSIC PLAYING]

